Introduction
The cycloidal rotor or cyclorotor has the particular feature of having its blades’ span direction aligned with the rotation axis. The working principle is shown in Fig. 1. Thrust is generated by harmonically pitching the blades, ensuring a positive angle of attack for the majority of the rotation. Despite being conceptualized over a century ago and recent industrial interest in cycloidal rotors, only limited test flights have been conducted thus far. To make cycloidal rotors industrially viable, their flight times need to be extended. To do this, their efficiency must be improved. Being efficient for a flying device capable of hover means that it has to be lightweight and has to efficiently use the area across which it propels the air through its rotor. For a cycloidal rotor, this area is calculated by rotor diameter times blade span. This area should also be kept small in order to ease flight in restrained zones. Researchers used two-dimensional (2D) computational fluid dynamics (CFD) to analyze different cyclorotor features such as the interactions between the blades (Ref. 1), alternative pitch functions (Ref. 2), airfoil shapes (Refs. 3, 4), the Magnus effect on the rotor axle (Ref. 5), virtual camber (Refs. 6–8), and, other dynamic effects (Ref. 9). The third spatial dimension becomes necessary to study tip vortices, out-of-plane inflow, turbulence, and aircraft structures. In addition, threedimensional (3D) CFD simulations are used to investigate the effects of blade endplates or rotor end-disks (Refs. 10, 11), the influence of blade planform taper and aspect ratios (Ref. 12), the spanwise thrust distribution (Refs. 13, 14), and guide the design of a drone (Refs. 15, 16). A case of using OpenFOAM’s PIMPLE algorithm with a 3D mesh to study a barebone cycloidal rotor system is also documented in Ref. 17. Additionally, numerous cyclorotors that have been constructed and tested are documented in the literature. Many of these tests were conducted in still
