Particular Aspects of Cyclorotors
Pitch control mechanism
Both uniform and nonuniform pitch mechanisms can be used to control cycloidal rotors. The uniform mechanism applies the classic motion of a cycloidal rotor and provides each blade of the rotor with the same pitching schedule. It is used, for example, in the prototype of Ref. 25. On the other hand, the nonuniform mechanism applies a slightly different pitch function to each blade of the rotor. An example of its use is seen in Ref. 26. In this work, a uniform mechanism was used. This mechanism is built into the prototype and shown in Fig. 16. The geometry of the uniform mechanism corresponds to using n linkages identical to the one for blade 0 shown in Figs. 1 and 2. The uniform pitch mechanism is geometrically simpler but arguably more complex to build than the nonuniform mechanism. This is caused by the pitch arms having to all meet at the pitch offset center point, which can also interfere with the rotor axle. In the prototype, this is achieved by distributing them along the control shaft and using a coupling ring shown in Fig. 16. This mechanism has the advantage of allowing each blade to follow the same pitching schedule φn(ψn) over a rotation, as shown in Fig. 3. The figure shows the pitch angle for different changes of the pitch link length l0. A positive φ means that the blade nose is moving away from the circle described by the blade pivot point. The linkage of the control mechanism forms a four-bar mechanism for each blade. The equations of each linkage were derived from several sources including Refs. 27 and 28. The pitch motion of each blade of the uniform mechanism is given by Eqs. (1)–(4)
