Virtual camber
In the flow field of a cycloidal rotor, both curvilinear flow and fast pitching occur. Both effects cause the symmetrical airfoil to behave as a cambered one would in rectilinear flow. The approximate direction and amount of camber are shown in Fig. 4. This is a known effect (Refs. 8,29), which is called the virtual camber and is briefly explained here. Looking at the blade in the position of Fig. 5, the upstroke pitching creates a downwards relative velocity component in front of the pivot point and an upwards velocity component behind it. Proceeding incrementally over the chord and computing the different inflow angles yields the resulting additional relative flow velocity vector. This vector can then be transformed into the virtual camber line of Fig. 5. The opposite occurs for downstroke pitching. The pitching rate being a function of the azimuth angle ψ causes the pitch-induced virtual camber effect to also depend on the blade’s azimuthal position. On the other hand, the virtual camber caused by curvilinear flow is constant over the rotation and is determined solely by the chord-to-radius ratio c/R0, which, however, should be corrected to account for the influence of the pivot point position on the mean radius of the blade path and its fixed or instantaneous pitch angle on the chord length projected along this path. It results from the relative velocity being tangent to the path of the blade. This relative flow velocity angle is zero at the pivot but changes along the chord. It thus increases in strength with c/R0. The same procedure as for pitching can be made by rotating the inflow vector for each chord element until the inflow is parallel to the original chord. This results in the camber line shown in Fig. 6. As a result of the virtual camber, at the two near-zero physical pitch angles occurring at ψ = −10◦ and ψ = 186◦, strongly differing pressure
distributions1 occur as shown in Figs. 7(a) and 7(b). In this figure, the inner side of the blade is the one facing the rotor center and rotation velocity is 5,300 RPM. The different pressure distributions at opposing positions can be explained by the two individual virtual cambers. At ψ = −10◦, the virtual camber due to curvilinear flow and virtual camber due to pitching velocity work in opposite directions. For the given geometric configuration, both effects have almost the same strength and cancel each other out. At ψ = 186◦, the virtual cambers resulting from curvilinear flow and pitching sum up and simulate a cambered profile. As a result, the pressure distribution along the chord differs as shown in Fig. 7(b). Although still disturbed by dynamic stall and the ensuing detaching vortex, the suction which occurs at the inner part of the symmetrical blade due to virtual camber is visible. Finally, Figs. 7(c) and 7(d) also show the influence of the virtual camber at ψ = 90◦ and ψ = 270◦. At those positions, the pitch velocity (φ ̇) is almost zero. For this reason, the virtual camber consists almost exclusively of the constant part from curvilinear flow. At ψ = 90◦, the virtual camber caused by rotation produces thrust in the negative y-direction, counteracting the thrust generated by the pitch angle of the blade. This leads, on the outer side of the airfoil, to a weaker negative pressure and a positive pressure at the rear half of the blade. At ψ = 270◦, pitching and virtual cambering act in the same direction. Hence, the whole inner side experiences a negative pressure and much more lift can be generated. The influence of the rotor-induced downflow reducing the effective angle of attack is at both positions important but is stronger at ψ = 270◦ because the vertical velocity increases from top to bottom.
