CFD Model
Each simulation in this study is conducted using the 3D URANS CFD method. The PIMPLE algorithm, an unsteady incompressible solver of the OpenFOAM finite volume software, is used. Turbulence is modeled by the k−ω−SST model implemented in OpenFOAM. The rotor arms and pitch links are neglected by the simulation, and thus only the blades of the cyclorotor are modeled. Furthermore, a symmetry boundary condition is used at midspan to reduce the computing costs by 50%.
Blade rotation and oscillation
Each rotor blade is located in a hemispherical grid zone, as shown in Fig. 9, which oscillates as a whole. Between these zones, the arbitrary mesh interface (AMI) boundary condition, represented by the yellow circle in Fig. 10, performs the conservative field interpolation of Ref. 30. The AMI interpolation weights are hereby computed for each timestep as a set of values between the matching cells of the inner and outer boundaries of the blade’s elongated hemisphere. The rotor rotation is applied simultaneously to every zone of the simulation domain around the rotor center. The pitch function of Eq. (4) is also applied individually to each elongated hemisphere blade zone around its center.
Boundary conditions and general parameters
Farfield-type boundary conditions are used for the outer bounds of the domain. The walls of the blades use their moving velocity and a nonslip condition made for grids having y+ < 1 and thus not relying on wall functions. A symmetry boundary condition acts at the midspan point of the whole simulation domain. The use of the PIMPLE algorithm allows running with a high Courant number because it conducts several layers of inner iterations at each timestep. A more thorough description of its implementation is available in Ref. 31. The limited linear scheme is used to solve the divergence operators, as it reaches second-order accuracy and limits to the upwind solution where the gradient is strong. A second-order backward time scheme is used. A fixed timestep corresponding to a rotation of 0.5◦ is used for the start and settlement phase of the rotor flow. The timestep is then gradually decreased to reach 0.1◦ for the last rotor revolutions, leading to a maximum Courant number of 30. Care must be taken when interpreting the Courant number because the PIMPLE algorithm uses several layers of inner iterations for each timestep. The results are then extracted from these final rotor revolutions. At the start of each simulation, the amplitude of the pitching function is ramped and the final iteration of each timestep is underrelaxed. To do so, the pitch angle of the mesh as calculated by the code is multiplied by (1 − cos( t))/2 for the first half rotation. After these initial adjustments, underrelaxation is only used for the inner iterations of the timesteps. To damp unsteady effects such as start vortices as the rotor begins to rotate, 12 rotations are run prior to conducting any evaluation of the results. Tests have shown that, by then, periodical constancy is reached and a fully developed flow regime is established, as shown in Fig. 8.
Mesh
By means of symmetry, the complete CFD domain is a hemisphere. A hybrid mesh consisting of structured and unstructured portions was used. The blade zones with their boundary layer extrusions are structured and the background mesh unstructured, but mostly Cartesian. Pointwise was used to generate the structured component meshes of the rotor blades, and the meshing guidelines of the drag prediction workshop (Ref. 32) were followed. The half rotor blades are modeled with 144 points along the
