Experimental Validation
Rotor prototype
The rotor consists of four cycloidal rotor blades, which have been 3D printed and utilize the conventional four-bar mechanism for pitch control. It was built with the intent to develop it into a two-rotor flying device which is not described in this paper but rather in Ref. 36. To withstand the centrifugal forces of two coaxial cyclorotors, the rotor arms are thicker on one side. Also, weight and size were kept small to ensure airworthiness and limit prototyping costs. Within these given constraints and to ensure maximal efficiency, the geometry of the rotor and the operating Reynolds number were strongly inspired by the results of the experimental study by Kellen et al. (Refs. 21,25). Consequently, the Reynolds number of 200,000 given in the reference study leads to a 5,300 RPM rotation velocity for the current prototype. This velocity proved to be a structural challenge, and thus the current prototype was only tested up to approximately 3,000 RPM. The rotor was designed with four NACA 0024 airfoils and is shown in Fig. 15 along with two detailed views in Figs 16 and 17. The rotor arms and blades were 3D printed using Z-ULTRAT acrylonitrile butadiene styrene based filament and experienced material shrinkage in the order of approximately 1%. The printer used is a Zortrax M200, and the blades were printed to have a single layer of material. Consequently, the printed blade thickness ratio changed from the planned 24% to 25.8%. This discrepancy between the designed and printed parts is the result of the plastic material shrinking as it cools. The same material, printer, and settings
