Measurement setup
The rotor was held on the test bench from both sides, as depicted in Fig. 18. Based on typical cycloidal rotor flows, it is known that the inflow is spread over a large angle and only the wake produces a concentrated jet that is likely to interact with any blocking object. Therefore, the possibility of blockage from the support arm and test rig can be safely neglected. This was also confirmed by the measurements and CFD of the flow around the rotor, as shown in Fig. 25. The pitch mechanism was adjusted to generate horizontal thrust, thereby mitigating ground effects. To do so, the offset (eccentric) link was placed directly downstream of the rotor. It thus had a zero angle with the negative y-axis; or in other words, an angle = 90◦. On the one side, the axle driving the pitch rods was connected to the electric motor, and, on the other side, the pitch rods were attached to a rigid frame. An offset between the rotor’s axis of rotation and the pitching was necessary to force the harmonic pitching of
