Limitations of the validation
Using a multibody dynamics model, an attempt was made to extract the inertia from the time signal of the forces and moments generated by the prototype rotor. To do this, the blade inertia was obtained by measuring its pendulum frequency and a simplified rotor model was constructed and used to identify the inertial forces. However, this attempt was not successful due to the presence of various nonharmonic vibration phenomena, including those associated with the test rig. This was the case even after filtering the experimental data with various band-pass filters. As a result, inertia was omitted because the high rotational frequency leads to nonlinear vibrations that cannot be filtered out by postprocessing. To ensure that inertial forces did not affect the measured force and moment, they were time-averaged over 50–400 revolutions, depending on the rotational speed. This method has been shown to give consistent results for a rotor using the same experimental equipment (Ref. 37). The
