force transducer of the experiment measures the total aerodynamic forces and moments acting on the rotor surfaces. As such, the globally measured total moment is not equal to the motor torque. The total measured torque consists of the motor torque which is transferred by the pivot rods and the additional moment transferred by the pitch rods. The pitch rod moment arises from the force the pitch rods transfer and their eccentric attachment. This moment is measured by the force transducer but is not relevant for the power calculation. Only the moment borne by the pivot rods is transferred to the rotor axle.
Parametric Study
This section reports and discusses the findings of the CFD-based parametric study. To provide a quick overview, the performance indicators CT , Cpow, and FoM are plotted in Fig. 26 for most of the investigated cases. It also includes simulations with two and three blades, the results of which can be summarized by saying that the thrust and lift per blade hardly changed, thus leading to a lower FoM.
Aspect ratio
As is the case for helicopter blades and aircraft fixed wings, cycloidal rotor blades develop tip vortices. These high-energy turbulent wake phenomena usually disturb the generation of lift. To prevent the consequent loss of energy efficiency, helicopters and aircraft have blades or wings with high aspect ratios. However, the blades of a cyclorotor are subjected to strong centrifugal forces, which limit the maximum allowable aspect ratio because they act perpendicular to the spanwise axis. The aspect ratio of the blades of the built prototype is b/c = 1.5. Using the CFD model, ratios between b/c = 1 and b/c = 6 were simulated. The plots of the thrust and power coefficients over a rotation are shown in Figs. 27 and 28. As expected, elongating the blade span was found to increase energy efficiency. While the default case has a FoM of 0.540, the case with a doubled blade span increases to a FoM of 0.596. Further increasing the aspect ratio to 6 raises efficiency only slightly to a FoM of 0.601. This agrees with prior findings from other researchers such as Ref. 39 who showed that the FoM of their cyclorotor increases by roughly 0.05 between an aspect ratio of 1 and 4 and then stays unchanged for an aspect ratio of 8. The aspect ratio of 3 yields a higher thrust coefficient and a similar power coefficient than the reference case. The aspect ratio of 6 generates the same absolute thrust per area as the default while requiring less power per area. Thus, reducing blade span increases power demand per unit area, while the thrust per area shows a maximum for b/c = 3, as reported in Fig. 26. This can also be observed in the evolution of the thrust and power coefficients over the azimuthal angle ψ, CT , and Cpow. The aspect ratio b/c = 3 case attains a comparable amplitude in CT to the default case but with a less pronounced manner. Consequently, the average thrust coefficient is higher, while the power coefficient maintains a very similar shape and average value. The thrust for the aspect ratio of 6 is a lower but longer-lasting peak in the CT curve when compared to the default case. Furthermore, an increased thrust at ψ = 0◦ is observable, while previous cases have most often shown hardly any generated thrust at this azimuth. The power coefficient Cpow is decreased at almost every azimuthal position, which seems plausible when expecting smaller tip vortex losses. The blade with a square geometry (b/c = 1) exhibits a lower thrust coefficient at ψ = 90◦ but a similar shape of the thrust coefficient curve in the lower half, compared to the default case. The power coefficient curve follows roughly the default case and peaks at ψ = 270◦. The rotor with an aspect ratio of b/c = 1 and the default case with an aspect ratio of 1.5 show a single horseshoe vortex forming on each blade
