Endplates
The addition of disks having a radius of rendplate = 140 mm and being spanwise coincident with the blade tips was studied. These disks or endplates were modeled without friction as slip walls. Adding them was expected to prevent the formation of blade tip vortices and thus increase efficiency. The simulation results confirmed this assumption as the endplates allowed the FoM to increase from 0.540 to 0.618 at 1,433 RPM and from 0.581 to 0.713 at 5,300 RPM. Since the endplates generate a much more 2D airflow regime, a comparison of both the 3D case without endplates and the 2D simulation was conducted. The azimuthal force and power distribution resulting from endplates resembled the 2D distribution much closer than the 3D case. The plots of the thrust and power coefficients over a rotation are shown in Figs. 31 and 32. It featured a large region of negative thrust for 135 ◦ ≤ ψ ≤ 200◦, positive thrust coupled to a negative power value at ψ = 0◦, and a highly disrupted thrust section in the second half of the revolution. A similarly disturbed 2D thrust curve was also observed by Ref. 40. With the endplates, thrust and power were reduced by 11% and 26%, respectively. This corresponds with the expectation that tip vortices
increase lift slightly while significantly increasing power. Thus, the case with endplates requires the least power while still generating a decent amount of thrust. Although tip vortices reduce the FoM, other 3D effects such as the turbulent horseshoe vortex appear to delay stall and consequently increase efficiency. As such, the simulation with endplates yields a better FoM than the corresponding 2D case. Reference 21 measured configurations with endplates to be approximately 2% more efficient, while the FoM increases by 14% in the current work, leaving out the results at 5,300 RPM. However, they used individual side disks at the end of each blade instead of one large disk for all blades for each side used in the current work. The increase in efficiency found by the present study for a rotor with endplates agrees with the findings of Ref. 10 but not with those of Ref. 17. Reference 17 obtained about 15% lower aerodynamic efficiencies for a rotor with endplates by optimizing their pitch schedule. Reference 10, instead, did something similar to the current study and did not run an optimization algorithm on the pitch schedule. Thus, they observed that the rotor with endplates behaved similarly to the 2D rotor and was more efficient. This difference with the former can be explained by the individual optimization of their pitch schedule, which in their case may lead to better handling of 3D effects, such as those hindering stall, and a better interaction between the upstream and downstream flow. It thus indicates that future studies should investigate the combined effects of a tuned pitch control algorithm both with and without the endplates. At the bottom of the rotation (ψ = 0◦), the inner surface experiences a suction peak, caused by lateral inwards inflow which shifts the angle of attack (AoA) into negative regions. The resulting force points inwards and upwards delivering positive thrust and requiring negative power. At the top of the rotation (ψ = 90◦), the outer