Rotor axle
The influence of the rotor central shaft was investigated using simulations at = 1433.3 RPM. The mesh near the axle was generated in pointwise, including the boundary layer mesh. Axle diameters dax of 4 and 8.2 mm, which are the experimental rotor’s dimensions, and 12 mm were simulated. As the axle disturbs the wake and is affected by drag from surface friction, a larger axle diameter was expected to decrease the generated thrust and increase the required power, with both effects decreasing efficiency. As a result of the simulations, increasing the axle diameter only marginally decreased the FoM. Even at the largest axle diameter, the generated thrust was only reduced by less than 0.4% compared to the case without an axle. The increase in required power was analogously smaller than 0.3%, resulting in a FoM decreased by less than 0.004. Hence the influence of an axle of these dimensions on the efficiency is deemed negligible.
Discussion and Conclusions
A 3D URANS CFD toolkit was developed and validated qualitatively and quantitatively using a cyclorotor rotor built at the institute. The importance of the pitch mechanism and the virtual camber phenomenon were analyzed and discussed. Then, a CFD parametric study covering blade aspect ratio, side disks, pitch mechanism, blade camber, and axle size was conducted. The thrust and power coefficients for all investigated parametric studies are plotted in Fig. 26 together with the FoM isolines. The study revealed that efficiency is significantly affected by the distribution of thrust between the upstream and downstream portions of the rotor cylinder. In addition, the optimum distribution does not necessarily correspond to a uniform thrust distribution. This distribution can be directly influenced by adjusting the PRL or the camber of the blade, as shown in Figs. 37–39. The presence of a central axle in the rotor negligibly affected the efficiency. Manipulating the pitch mechanism can increase thrust and power to a large extent, and a longer pitch rod is in general more efficient than cambering the airfoils. The study of the effect of camber indicated that negative camber is unsuitable if the rotor is designed strictly for maximum hovering aerodynamic efficiency. However, the optimal positive camber only slightly increased the FoM while a −12% camber increased the thrust by approximately 35%. By using a −12% cambered airfoil, the same amount of thrust can be achieved by either a smaller blade area or a reduced rotational velocity. Both could lead to significant structural advantages due to a reduced blade mass and bending moment or to a reduced centripetal force for the same disk loading. In this study, the strongest observed impact on efficiency came from the side disks which behaved similarly to winglets on fixed wings. In addition to the observed positive impact, these endplates could cover the rotor and control rods to reduce parasitic aerodynamic losses. Nonetheless, changing the blade aspect ratios did show similar responses as for wings but with a less important impact, making it thinkable to fly a squarebladed cyclorotor even without side disks. Decreasing the blade aspect ratio worsened the rotor efficiency but in a surprisingly limited fashion. The reduced structural requirements associated with the reduced bending moment of a square blade would result in lighter blades. Overall, the sensibility of the rotor to different parameters was highlighted by the range of encountered FoMs going from 0.298 for a 78-mm long pitch rod to 0.713 for the four-blade rotor with endplates at 5,300 RPM. Furthermore, the simulations also showed that the FoM continuously increased with the rotation velocity, probably due to Reynolds number effects. A torque correction was applied to all moments, powers, and FoMs resulting from the parametric study. In doing so, only the actual power delivered by the motor was considered necessary, and the moment and
resulting power coming from the force channeled through the pitching arm was not considered. Thus, the force required to hold the pitch control shaft in place was assumed to be negligible. There are many scenarios where this would be the case, such as the experimental test rig in this study where the pitch control shaft was physically blocked. It should be noted, however, that some flying prototypes may require servomotor power to achieve pitch control. Although a comprehensive study of various parameters was conducted, it remains clear that agreement with the research of other studies cannot always be reached. This is often due to the difference in observed Reynolds numbers but also comes from a more complex combination of parameters such as airfoil type, aspect ratio, pitch mechanism, chordto-radius ratio, and more. This indicates that it will eventually be desirable to establish a reference cyclorotor geometry on which the research tools and findings of different working groups may be benchmarked and validated.
